/* book exam traps: Using degrees instead of radians: cos(1.195°) ≈ 0.9998 gives sec ≈ 1.00 and σmax ≈ 48 MPa, not 61.8 MPa. The sec argument is in radians. | Dropping the leading 1 in the bracket: σmax is not (P/A)(ec/r²)sec alone. Without the direct-stress term the example gives only about 21.8 MPa of bending stress. | Mixing e and c: The product ec is symmetric, so this example gives the same number if you swap them — but the two are not interchangeable. c is a section property (centroid to extreme fibre, 10 mm here), e is the load offset (2 mm here). Confusing them gives w | Using L instead of Le: for fixed ends Le = 0.5L, so the sec argument is halved and the secant is much smaller. Check the end condition before substituting. | Applying the elastic secant formula when σmax exceeds the proportional limit. Then Et < E and the real stress is larger; see inelastic buckling. */
