/* book: the chapter, paragraph by paragraph (first sentences):
   - Slenderness is λ = Le/r, the effective length over the radius of gyration, as in Euler buckling.
   - The proportional limit σprop is the highest stress at which the stress-strain curve is still a straight line.
   - At a working stress σ, the tangent modulus Et is the slope dσ/dε of the stress-strain curve at that point.
   - Pcr is the inelastic buckling load (N); Et is the tangent modulus at the buckling stress (N/mm²); I is the least second moment of area (mm⁴); Le is the effective length (mm).
   - The switch from Euler to tangent modulus occurs at the proportional-limit slenderness λp.
   - λp is the proportional-limit slenderness (dimensionless); E is Young's modulus (N/mm²); σprop is the proportional limit (N/mm²).
   - For λ ≥ λp, Euler is valid: σcr = π²E/λ².
   - Kármán refined Engesser's model.
   - Shanley settled which modulus starts the buckle.
   - For design, the conservative and simple choice is Et.
*/
